<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What is the difference between facial pain and facial swelling from infection versus TMJ?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">TMJ pain is typically aching or clicking pain in front of the ear that worsens with jaw movement and eating. TMJ does not cause redness or skin warmth. An infectious/inflammatory cause produces redness, warmth, visible swelling, and often fever. Our examination differentiates these based on the location of pain, presence of swelling or redness, and jaw mobility.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How quickly can facial pain and swelling from infection be treated?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Abscess drainage provides immediate pain relief — patients typically feel better within hours of drainage. Antibiotics for cellulitis begin showing results within 24–48 hours. Complete resolution of swelling takes 7–14 days. Source treatment (extraction or root canal) is scheduled within the same visit or follow-up to prevent recurrence.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can facial swelling and pain indicate something serious?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — facial pain and swelling together can be a sign of a spreading infection that may involve deep facial spaces, the orbit, or the airway. Rapid worsening of facial swelling, difficulty swallowing, or breathing problems are medical emergencies. Even without these red flags, the underlying cause (often a dental abscess) should be treated promptly to prevent recurrence and complications.</div>
</details>
</div>
